WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.630
Modern trolls lurk about social media, harassing
and antagonizing, disguising their identity

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:10.750
and hiding in the dark edges of the internet…
the namesake of this bad behavior makes more

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:15.570
sense when you learn all about the early tales
and evolution of this creature.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:21.390
A Nordic creature based in Scandinavian legend,
many trolls appear in the Icelandic sagas.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:26.119
And originally, the troll was less a certain
type of monster and more a term used to refer

00:00:26.119 --> 00:00:27.540
to a variety of monsters.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:32.390
It was more about what a creature did that
made it a troll—not its appearance.

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:36.430
A lot like our modern day trolls.

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:40.989
[Monstrum intro.]

00:00:40.989 --> 00:00:44.280
Before the 19th century, ‘troll’ was a
massively broad term.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:49.180
Part of the reason it’s so hard to define
a troll is that we still aren’t even sure

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:52.059
how the word developed or its original meaning.

00:00:52.059 --> 00:00:56.990
The word ‘troll’ in Old Norse-Icelandic
could have indicated huge creatures, giants,

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:01.829
and Titans but most likely just meant some
kind of ‘magical being.”

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:08.060
In Old Norse, it even has two forms and both
have a myriad of meanings—but all indicate

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:09.060
malevolence.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:14.439
While in modern-day stories, trolls are typically
portrayed as lacking intelligence, but ancient

00:01:14.439 --> 00:01:16.340
trolls were actually quite clever.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:20.859
The earliest stories of trolls date back to
1220–1230 CE.

00:01:20.859 --> 00:01:25.950
In Snorri Sturluson’s Prose Edda we find—Bragi the Old, a fictional counterpart

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:30.840
of a human poet of the same name who lived
in Norway during the late ninth century and

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:34.399
who had a chance encounter with a female troll.

00:01:34.399 --> 00:01:38.029
Bragi came across the troll while
driving some kind of sled or wagon through

00:01:38.029 --> 00:01:39.960
the forest at night.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:44.849
Instead of greeting the man with threats of
violence—she introduces herself in a poetic

00:01:44.849 --> 00:01:50.350
voice as “moon of the dwelling-Rungnir,
giant’s wealth-sucker, storm-sun’s bale,

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:56.420
seeress’s friendly companion, guardian of
corpse-fjord, swallower of heaven wheel.”

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:00.729
The poet responds with his own introduction
and the encounter ends.

00:02:00.729 --> 00:02:05.719
By letting the troll speak first and mirroring
her speech, the poet shows deference to the

00:02:05.719 --> 00:02:08.250
troll, indicating he recognizes the land as
hers.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:14.510
From her introduction we learn a few things:
she’s wealthy, practices magic, and can

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:16.870
bring about death and destruction.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:20.629
These traits would form the backbone of troll
mythos.

00:02:20.629 --> 00:02:23.299
This is just one troll tale of many.

00:02:23.299 --> 00:02:28.290
It should be stressed that ancient trolls
weren’t a clearly defined being . In their

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:33.269
earliest appearances in folklore and legends,
a troll was an evil paranormal being that

00:02:33.269 --> 00:02:37.990
occupied the space outside of civilization
and posed a threat to the living things within

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:38.990
it.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:42.989
Trolls often lurked at the boundaries of the
community—as beings who inhabited areas

00:02:42.989 --> 00:02:46.560
away from the farms and communal spaces of
humans.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:49.530
Their presence marked danger and often death.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:54.440
They were anti-social creatures that could
be any kind of monster from witches to vampires,

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:56.609
possessed animals to ghosts.

00:02:56.609 --> 00:03:01.400
They could be large giants or diminutive elves,
were often skilled at magic, hoarded gold

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:05.110
and other treasures, and were incredibly strong.

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:09.299
Perhaps because of the early Braggi example—and
the general belief that women were more skilled

00:03:09.299 --> 00:03:14.440
at magic, female trolls are considered particularly
evil.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.780
Another saga [Arrow-Oddr’s Saga] describes
the female troll Ogmundr as”the greatest

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:22.060
troll and monster ever born in the northern
regions of the world.”

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:26.219
This troll has a human head, fangs, a tail,
and talons.

00:03:26.219 --> 00:03:30.560
She appears to be a more than proficient fighter,
wielding dual blades and taking out sixty

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.890
men with surprising speed.

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:36.799
She is defeated only with a magic shirt and
dwarf-made arrow.

00:03:36.799 --> 00:03:41.560
Troll appearances in early 13th-century literature
add to the mutability of the creature.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:43.810
Sometimes they were said to be smaller than
most children.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:49.019
In other accounts, they could shapeshift into
animals and trees as well as nonliving objects.

00:03:49.019 --> 00:03:51.330
Trolls don’t have to be humanoid.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:55.279
In the late-13th century poem, “The Sybil’s
Prophecy” (Völuspá), which was likely

00:03:55.279 --> 00:04:00.359
recorded even earlier, a wolf is described
as “the chewer of the moon in the guise

00:04:00.359 --> 00:04:01.829
of a troll.”

00:04:01.829 --> 00:04:04.310
Trolls can appear individually, or in groups.

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:07.489
Like when in another Old Norse saga [Óláfs
saga Tryggvasonar] two men of a king’s retinue

00:04:07.489 --> 00:04:11.620
stumble upon a mountain cave with a group
of trolls around a fire.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:15.139
The trolls are discussing their fears that
the king will attack their homes and drive

00:04:15.139 --> 00:04:19.690
them away--threatening the families and cultures
they are trying to protect.

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:22.800
But still, the narrator of the story calls
them “demons.”

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.940
Again, this seems to be the general basis
for calling something a troll—the threat

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:29.380
of violence.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:33.880
As one 13th century poet writes “not much
is worse than trolls.”

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:38.030
Since the publication of the sagas in the
Middle Ages, trolls have lingered in the popular

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:41.770
imagination, most notably in the Scandinavian
countries.

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:45.350
Medieval Norwegian law even recognized the
existence of trolls.

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:50.590
Between the 12th and 14th centuries, laws
made it illegal to “[sit] outside to awaken

00:04:50.590 --> 00:05:00.720
trolls in order to perform heathenism” or
to call someone a troll or a witch.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.009
often, Medieval trolls are threatening.

00:05:05.009 --> 00:05:09.830
In part because trolls marked the outside
boundary of the rural community—beings who

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:15.340
lived away from the farm and communal spaces
humans inhabited aka so-called “wild”

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:16.340
creatures.

00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:20.830
In addition to their tendencies to steal objects
and land another bit of famous troll lore

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:23.479
is their penchant for stealing people.

00:05:23.479 --> 00:05:28.169
Trolls usually kidnap female victims, likely
because Nordic beliefs of the time were that

00:05:28.169 --> 00:05:33.410
at certain periods in a woman’s life, during
childbirth or right after it, they were considered

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:36.919
“outside” of the Church and therefore
more vulnerable.

00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:38.810
But men weren’t necessarily safe.

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:44.260
In a 14th century saga a young man is kidnapped
by a hideous female troll.

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:50.289
She is as small as a child but incredibly
stout, with a long face, crooked nose, black

00:05:50.289 --> 00:05:51.669
hair—and a black skull.

00:05:51.669 --> 00:05:55.270
The man must kiss her, despite her dripping
nose.

00:05:55.270 --> 00:06:00.210
When he wakes up in her bed, he is surprised
to see his beautiful fiancé lying next to

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:03.539
him—her “troll woman skin” at the foot
of the bed.

00:06:03.539 --> 00:06:07.949
The woman’s stepmother had turned her into
a troll with a curse—but her fiancé’s

00:06:07.949 --> 00:06:09.819
kiss broke the spell.

00:06:09.819 --> 00:06:12.509
And yes—the story seemed familiar to me
too.

00:06:12.509 --> 00:06:16.330
It seems like Shrek has some grounding in
ancient Nordic tradition.

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:21.550
These early troll tales laid the foundation,
but the 16th century saw the biggest change

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:23.419
in troll folklore.

00:06:23.419 --> 00:06:29.000
The mid-16th century marks the religious Reformation
of Scandinavia—when Lutheranism became the

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:30.979
dominant religion.

00:06:30.979 --> 00:06:35.280
During this period of religious focus, trolls
were associated less with the supernatural

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:39.849
and more with the Devil although that connection
would fade with time (and a waning belief

00:06:39.849 --> 00:06:41.240
in witches).

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:45.330
In an attempt to quell the legends of trolls
and other supernatural creatures, a collection

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:50.770
of Christian hymns was published in Iceland,
hoping to replace the pagen notions with formalized

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:51.770
religion.

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:55.350
The text’s [Sálmabók] introduction states
that the “unprofitable songs of trolls and

00:06:55.350 --> 00:07:00.470
of the [pagan] peoples of old” are in the
service of the Devil and are of a “Heathen”

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:01.470
tradition.

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:05.400
This post-Reformation period is also when
we see more stories about trolls who wish

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:07.789
to convert to Christianity.

00:07:07.789 --> 00:07:12.629
In Scandinavian, and Irish tradition as well,
fairy and elf lore includes stories of the

00:07:12.629 --> 00:07:16.780
fae going to mass and participating in Christian
rituals.

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:18.699
Trolls could become Christians.

00:07:18.699 --> 00:07:21.560
Even a malevolent beast can be reformed.

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:26.520
One Icelandic folktale “Goldbrow and Skeggi
in Hvammur” tells of a greedy and evil troll

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:27.520
named Gullbrá.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:32.240
Having set her sights on a particular parcel
of land, she uses witchcraft to appear as

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:37.350
a young and beautiful woman and tricks a man
into selling the land of his bedridden boss,

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:42.930
Auður [Atha], a well-known female settler
and political leader —who also just so happens

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:46.689
to be one of the few Christians in the overwhelmingly
pagan country.

00:07:46.689 --> 00:07:52.020
The troll builds a pagan temple on the land
Auður intended to preserve as sacred space.

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:54.419
But Gullbrá’s success is short-lived.

00:07:54.419 --> 00:07:58.560
Driven off the land, Gullbra’s attempts
to flee with her gold, blindfolding herself

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:02.009
to avoid looking at the crosses that would
forbid her from leaving.

00:08:02.009 --> 00:08:07.449
But when her treasure tumbles the greedy monster
hastily rips off her blindfold, and in doing

00:08:07.449 --> 00:08:09.639
so is exposed to the crosses.

00:08:09.639 --> 00:08:12.120
Her eyes are horrifically burned.

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:15.000
She escapes but dies from the wounds.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:18.240
But death is no burden for our good lady-troll.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:22.969
She rises as a ghost, killing animals and
a couple humans, behavior behavior a famous

00:08:22.969 --> 00:08:29.060
Nordic pagan Skeggi aattempts to stop—his
first battle against the troll is unsuccessful

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:33.650
and a few days later, he tries again, this
time He defeats the troll and takes her gold

00:08:33.650 --> 00:08:35.950
but is gravely injured.

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:40.160
.. On his deathbed, Skeggi asks that part
of the treasure is used to build a church

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:44.970
because the pagan gods did not help him in
his time of need, but the Christian god did,

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:47.900
by turning the troll to stone.

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:52.880
Despite their pagan association and malevolent
reputation, trolls came to be known for building

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:53.880
churches.

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:56.280
In a 1654 account (Jens Lauritzon Wolf’s
Encomion regni Daniae) of the construction

00:08:56.280 --> 00:09:01.980
of Lund Cathedral by St. Lawrence, a troll
offers his services—on the condition that

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:07.040
he would be paid with the sun and the moon,
or the eyes of the saint, unless St. Lawrence

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:08.700
could guess his name.

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:11.030
Surprisingly, the deal was struck.

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:15.840
The lucky saint overhears the troll’s wife
say the builder’s name—Finn.

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:20.490
Upon hearing his name uttered, the troll grasps
one of the church’s foundational stone pillars

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:22.720
and tries to topple the building.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:28.870
The saint averts the collapse by transforming
the troll, his wife, and their child to stone--just

00:09:28.870 --> 00:09:30.460
like God had done to Gullbra.

00:09:30.460 --> 00:09:35.070
If you go to Lund Cathedral in Sweden today,
you can see a giant figure carved into the

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:40.350
stone—although scholars now identify the
carving as the Biblical Samson.

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:44.790
Given that St. Lawrence was martyred in Rome
centuries before Christianity reached Sweden,

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:49.600
and you know, the whole troll thing, this
story was likely fabricated to give local

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:52.800
context to the unusual carving.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:57.660
Legends of similarly greedy troll church builders
exist in Denmark and Norway as well.

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:02.420
If you ask me, I believe that the troll as
builder legend was a way to keep ancient Nordic

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:08.330
influences alive post-Christianity when stories
were introduced to try and reshape pagen and

00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:09.650
ancient traditions.

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:14.030
If I’ve learned anything from my research
for ‘Monstrum’ it’s that cultures will

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:19.790
try to keep nods and winks to their ancient
monsters—despite all the odds.

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:23.060
Other support for this theory is found in
an ancient Nordic myth about the building

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:27.370
of a wall or palace around Asgard, the home
of the gods.

00:10:27.370 --> 00:10:33.070
The man who takes on the task asks for a payment
of the sun and the moon--and the goddess Freya.

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:37.240
Luckily for her, the man is tricked by the
God of Mischief, Loki, into not completing

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:40.040
the task by the assigned time.

00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:45.820
In his rage the man began smashing the stones
around him—revealing himself to be a giant—a

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:47.720
foe of the Nordic gods.

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:51.300
The Thunder God Thor kills the giant with
his hammer.

00:10:51.300 --> 00:10:56.400
Like many ancient beliefs Troll legends began
to fade as science progressed and formal education

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:01.580
became commonplace —that is until the folklore
fascination of the 19th century.

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:07.230
The Famous Danish author Hans Christian Andersen
included a princess taking a troll lover in

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:11.920
his first-ever published fairy-tale in 1830
(“The Dead Man”), marking the troll’s

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:13.880
entrance into fantasy literature.

00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:21.280
In 1852, the “T-R-O-L-L” spelling appears
in English for the first time in Benjamin

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:24.310
Thorpe’s Scandinavian Popular Traditions
and Superstitions.

00:11:24.310 --> 00:11:30.450
And around that time, troll illustrations
began to accompany folklore and fairy tales,

00:11:30.450 --> 00:11:34.830
giving us some of the first and lasting ideas
of a troll’s appearance.

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:39.750
The very first illustration, sketched by Johan
Fredrik Eckersberg in 1850 [for Asbjørnsen’s

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:46.050
book] is a hairy, ugly, bearded, troll with
a large nose— and honestly, that’s a foundational

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:48.310
image we still draw from.

00:11:48.310 --> 00:11:53.890
That same book, by Norwegian folklorist Peter
Christen Asbjørnsen, is the first publication

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:58.500
of the ‘Three Billy Goats Gruff” story,
where a long-nosed troll tries to eat three

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:04.150
goats as they cross a bridge before being
defeated by the third and largest billy goat—a

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:06.290
story that lives on today.

00:12:06.290 --> 00:12:11.190
Another Norwegian illustrator, Theodor Kittelsen,
produced some of the most influential (and

00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:15.330
striking) images of trolls in the later part
of the 19th century.

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:19.710
His trolls vary in size and appearance, but
their bodies always blur and blend with their

00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:25.070
surroundings—implying that the border of
the supernatural and natural are nebulous,

00:12:25.070 --> 00:12:28.130
and perhaps giving a little nod to their one-time
shapeshifting capabilities.

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:33.540
The middle of the century also marked the
first time trolls were taxonomized into their

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:36.010
own “monster” category.

00:12:36.010 --> 00:12:40.870
The first written collection of Icelandic
folktales was published by Jón Árnason and

00:12:40.870 --> 00:12:43.910
Magnús Grímsson in 1852 (Icelandic Adventure
Tales/Íslenzkar þjóðsögur og ævintýri].

00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:47.940
Árnason, like many other folklorists of the
century, was influenced by the scientific

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:52.730
classification system that was on the rise;
they attempted to divide the supernatural

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:55.710
into ‘species’ and categories.

00:12:55.710 --> 00:13:01.980
He essentially divided elves, trolls, ghosts,
and other monsters into their own categories

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:07.180
when before there had been greater overlap,
especially when it came to refer to a supernatural

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:09.450
or evil creature as a “troll.”

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:15.000
In 1867, famous playwright Henrik Ibsen featured
trolls in Peer Gynt [Gint], and invented a

00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:19.130
new aspect of troll lore——the troll king.

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:25.120
First performed in 1876 the play would become
Norway’s most performed play of all time.

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:28.310
Even today there is a Peer [Gint] festival
every year.

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:32.990
At the turn of the century, 19th century fairy
tale trolls moved to novels— notably into

00:13:32.990 --> 00:13:35.100
science fiction and fantasy.

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:36.630
In the 1950s, J.R.R.

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:39.240
Tolkien really delivered on the troll.

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:43.190
In The Hobbit and his Lord of the Rings series,
one of the most successful literary series

00:13:43.190 --> 00:13:49.230
of all time, Tolkien includes warrior trolls,
stone-trolls, hill-trolls, cave-trolls, and

00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:50.520
mountain-trolls.

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:54.710
And fun fact: in the Norwegian translation
of The Lord of the Rings, Gandalf the Wizard

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:56.300
is called ‘the troll-man’ (trollmannen).

00:13:56.300 --> 00:14:00.740
Given that Tolkien was both an Old English
and Old Norse scholar, I think he’d be pretty

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:02.070
chuffed about that.

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:07.500
The fantasy table-top game Dungeons &amp; Dragons
released in 1974 included the troll as one

00:14:07.500 --> 00:14:12.550
of its first monsters—further solidifying
the image of a green-skinned troll in the

00:14:12.550 --> 00:14:14.030
fantasy genre.

00:14:14.030 --> 00:14:17.790
We also can’t talk about modern trolls without
talking about Troll Dolls.

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:21.780
Yes, those odd, child-like figurines with
jewel belly buttons.

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:26.520
While the exact origins of the first troll
doll are somewhat dubious, it is generally

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:32.250
believed Thomas Dam carved the first wooden
troll doll in Denmark in the 1940s post World

00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:33.250
War II.

00:14:33.250 --> 00:14:37.360
The dolls became locally popular and eventually,
Dam was commissioned to carve a Santa Claus

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:39.590
for a Swedish department store.

00:14:39.590 --> 00:14:43.110
The display also included elf troll dolls.

00:14:43.110 --> 00:14:48.870
Long story short, everyone loved the little
things and in 1961 he mass-produced the crazy-haired,

00:14:48.870 --> 00:14:53.810
plastic version we now know and love—or
find inherently creepy if you were me as a

00:14:53.810 --> 00:14:54.810
child.

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:59.540
The immensely popular 2016 animated movie
inspired by the dolls introduced a whole new

00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:05.210
generation of parents and children to the
creatures—and made trolls popular again.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:07.870
But today, trolls aren’t always so fun and
colorful.

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:13.140
They appear in horror literature and film
often as threatening monsters and as a manifestation

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:14.780
of foreignness.

00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:20.150
1986’s Troll horror-comedy follows a fairy
who was forcefully transformed into a troll

00:15:20.150 --> 00:15:25.730
as he uses magic to try and eliminate all
humans by turning them into mythical creatures.

00:15:25.730 --> 00:15:30.450
The 2010’s Norwegian film Trollhunter, alludes
to many of the old legends [billy goat bridge

00:15:30.450 --> 00:15:33.960
scene] in a modernist retelling where trolls
have been forced to hide in the woods by the

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:35.330
Norwegian government.

00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:41.560
In 2018’s fantasy romance Border, trolls
live among humans, ostracized for their looks,

00:15:41.560 --> 00:15:43.670
behavior, and intersexuality.

00:15:43.670 --> 00:15:48.380
From Nordic sagas to Medieval law to those
ugly little dolls, and of course, hateful

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:53.970
online Internet users who lurk in comments—trolls
have been around for more than a thousand

00:15:53.970 --> 00:15:54.970
years.

00:15:54.970 --> 00:15:58.650
While they haven’t always looked the same,
the malleability of their original monstrosity

00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:23.360
has allowed them 

00:16:23.360 --> 00:16:30.230
to endure.

